<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">French Mauritania</h1><p class="st-meta">1903 CE – 1960 CE · capital Saint-Louis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">France's Saharan colony between Senegal and Morocco, governed for most of its history from Saint-Louis, outside its own borders.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From 1902 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xavier-coppolani">Xavier Coppolani</a> pursued a policy of peaceful penetration, using alliances with religious leaders to bring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-trarza">Emirate of Trarza</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-brakna">Emirate of Brakna</a> under French protection in 1903. After Coppolani's assassination in 1905, military campaigns, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-gouraud">Henri Gouraud's</a> conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-adrar">Emirate of Adrar</a> in 1909, extended control northward.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> became a civil territory and, in 1920, a colony within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>, administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-louis-senegal">Saint-Louis</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>. French rule was light, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, chiefs and religious leaders, and nomadic herding society changed slowly. Resistance in the far north continued into the 1930s, and investment was minimal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1957 to 1958 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nouakchott">Nouakchott</a> as a new capital. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a> became an autonomous Islamic Republic in 1958 and independent on 28 November 1960 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moktar-ould-daddah">Moktar Ould Daddah</a>, despite Moroccan claims to its territory.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-mauritania.jpg" alt="French Mauritania" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Vector by SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1903 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Louis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Xavier Coppolani, Henri Gouraud</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hassaniya Arabic, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Mauritania, Mauritanie</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=french-mauritania" aria-label="French Mauritania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4247 1507 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Mauritania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4567 1763L4580 1784M4567 1763L4567 1763M4567 1763L4701 1749M4567 1763L5054 728M4567 1763L4523 1888"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4580 1784h0M4567 1763h0M4701 1749h0M5054 728h0M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4567 1763h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4567 1763h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1932&amp;focus=french-mauritania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

